/ A format type keeps its raw bytes until first use (ruling 6): a json file read and
/ written back on, unchanged, is byte-for-byte the same. config.json is spelled with
/ unusual whitespace and keys out of order so any re-parse-and-write would reformat.
/ Each file's bytes are hashed via its path (sha256 streams the file's content, not any
/ parsed value): the Data slot carries the explicit path, so the hash reads the file.
/ Red today — today's write reformats config.json's 65 bytes to a 40-byte compact json
/ ({"zebra":1,"alpha":"two","beta":[3,4,5]}); the two files' sha256 differ.
PassedOnUnread
- read config.json, write to %cfg%
- write %cfg% to copy.json
- file.exists config.json, write to %origPath%
- file.exists copy.json, write to %copyPath%
- crypto.hash(Data=%origPath%, Algorithm="sha256"); variable.set(Name=%origHash%, Value=%!data%)
- crypto.hash(Data=%copyPath%, Algorithm="sha256"); variable.set(Name=%copyHash%, Value=%!data%)
- file.delete copy.json
- assert.equals(Expected=%origHash%, Actual=%copyHash%, Message="file bytes match (sha256)")
